<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Sudovians</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 1283 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Warlike West Baltic people of the lake and forest country between the Neman and Narew, who held off Rus', Poland and the Teutonic Order for centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ptolemy's 'Sudinoi' in the 2nd century are usually taken to be the Sudovians, also called Yotvingians. They occupied the lands around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwalki">Suwałki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> and southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>, close kin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/galindians">Galindians</a>, and were organised as a loose federation of territories led by hillfort chieftains rather than a single state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their economy rested on farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, hunting and the amber and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, supplemented by raiding. They kept Baltic pagan cults and a language close to Old Prussian. Rus' princes campaigned against them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great's</a> expedition of 983, and Polish dukes repeatedly invaded in the 12th and 13th centuries; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste">Bolesław V the Chaste</a> killed the chief Komata in 1264.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1274 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> turned on Sudovia, and in 1283 its last war leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skomantas">Skomantas</a>, submitted. Many Sudovians were resettled in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sambians">Sambia</a>, in a district long called the Sudauer Winkel, while others fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. The emptied land became a frontier wilderness, and the language died out.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sudovians.jpg" alt="Sudovians" width="320" height="277"><figcaption>Image: Unochepassava94 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yotvingian_Tribes_12th_century-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 1283 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Skomantas, Komata</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baltic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sudovian (Yotvingian)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>, <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yotvingians, Jatvingians, Sudovia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=692&amp;focus=sudovians" aria-label="Sudovians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5116 394 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sudovians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5451 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5436 650h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5436 650h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Poland. <a href="/atlas?year=692&amp;focus=sudovians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

